# S1-S2: brain from engine, shots as they are

A core with no Robocode, then an honest picture of the enemy

Unit 1, talk 3

---

## S1: the goal

Move all of Hadur's strategy into a core with **no Robocode imports**, without changing how it plays.

Everything after S1 depends on it: strategy can be tested, replayed and reasoned about outside the engine.

---

## The hexagon in one picture

```mermaid
graph LR
    E[Robocode engine] -->|events| A[Adapter]
    A -->|BotInput| C[Core]
    C -->|BotOrders| A
    A -->|setters| E
    C -.->|line records| T[Telemetry port]
```

ArchUnit enforces the boundary on every build.

---

## Two safety nets (S1)

- **Guard (RES-1):** if the core throws, the robot still gets safe orders that tick. No faults in **1,050** benchmarked rounds.
- **Replay (CORE-2):** recorded battles replay through a fresh core to the live robot's exact orders, bit for bit. An unintended change in play fails the build.

---

## The faithful port paid off

Comparing same-seed battles against 1.20 turn by turn exposed two slips:

1. 1.20's fire check reads the gun's remaining turn in degrees, not radians
2. 1.20's aim sees gun heat from a shot fired earlier in the same tick

After the fixes, 3 of the 4 comparable battles were identical for all 35 rounds.

---

## S1 result: no change, as intended

- Shadow score share **46.2% ± 6.4**, 98 of 175 rounds
- Within noise of S0 (40.8% ± 8.3)
- The gate was "do not change play", and the numbers say it did not

---

## S2: see the enemy's bullets as they really are

- 1.20 treated *any* energy drop as a shot
- S2 adds an **energy ledger** (WAVE-1, WAVE-2)
- Between scans it subtracts our bullet damage, the enemy's hit refund, collisions and inferred wall damage
- Only a remainder in [0.1, 3.0] becomes a wave

---

## S2 result: waves that exist

- Against Shadow, **99.4%** of the shots a scan can reveal become waves, with **0** false waves
- Across all opponents, **1,820** drops that 1.20 would have surfed as phantom waves were explained away
- On the samples, 8 of 12,144 inferred waves were false

---

## The bench told us what to fix

Wave fidelity was scored against the engine's real bullets:

1. 0.1-power shots read as 0.0999999, so compare with a tolerance
2. The engine applies acceleration before its wall check (13.6% false waves against Crazy)
3. The bench's own accounting was wrong too

Also found: after a skipped turn the radar could lose the enemy for the rest of the round (RADAR-1 fixed it).

---

## S2 result: score still flat

- Shadow **43.7% ± 4.6**, 93 of 175 rounds
- Every one of the 875 sample-bot rounds won
- Skipped turns **73 to 57**
- Honest reading: a better picture of the enemy has not yet changed the score

---

## What it taught us

- A boundary you can enforce (ArchUnit) is worth more than one you remember
- A faithful port, compared turn by turn, catches slips that tests do not
- Score from truth logs, not from belief: the bench's own accounting was wrong three times
- Flat results are results. S1 and S2 were meant to be flat or small.

---

